NAME
       shadow - the file of password hashes and password aging

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/shadow

DESCRIPTION
       /etc/shadow holds the password hash and the aging rules of each local
       account. Only root can read it. The nine fields are separated by
       colons:

           NAME:HASH:LAST:MIN:MAX:WARN:INACTIVE:EXPIRE:RESERVED

       NAME      the login name (as in /etc/passwd).
       HASH      the hashed password. On RHEL 9 it starts with $6$
                 (SHA-512). ! or !! (alone or in front of a hash) means
                 locked or never set: no password login is possible. An empty
                 field means no password is needed (never do that).
       LAST      the day of the last password change, counted in days since
                 1970-01-01. 0 means the user must change it at the next
                 login.
       MIN       days that must pass before the password may be changed
                 again.
       MAX       days a password stays valid; afterwards it must be changed.
       WARN      days before expiry that the user is warned.
       INACTIVE  days after expiry during which a login (with a forced
                 change) is still allowed; then the account is disabled.
       EXPIRE    the day (since 1970) the account stops working.
       RESERVED  unused.

       Empty aging fields mean "no limit". Change them with chage or passwd,
       and read them in plain words with chage -l NAME.

EXAMPLES
           harry:$6$Sx...:20514:0:90:7:::

           chage -l harry
           chage -M 90 -W 7 harry
           chage -d 0 harry      (force a change at the next login)

SEE ALSO
       passwd(5), chage(1), passwd(1), login.defs(5), usermod(8)
